###  https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/neo#StructureFailure
:StructureFailure rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf :ObjectFailure ;
                         <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "Loss of the ability of a structure to function within acceptance criteria. The structure is considered to fail when it becomes incapable of functioning, whether or not this is needed at that time."@en ;
                         <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "StructureFailure"@en .

[ rdf:type owl:Axiom ;
   owl:annotatedSource :StructureFailure ;
   owl:annotatedProperty <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> ;
   owl:annotatedTarget "Loss of the ability of a structure to function within acceptance criteria. The structure is considered to fail when it becomes incapable of functioning, whether or not this is needed at that time."@en ;
   :referenceNSSG "https://vocabulary.iaea.org/iaea-safety-glossary/949.html"^^xsd:anyURI
 ] .


###  https://w3id.org/emmo/domain/neo#MainSteamIsolationValve
:MainSteamIsolationValve rdf:type owl:Class ;
                         rdfs:subClassOf :ReactorProtectionSystem ,
                                         :PipePlantValve ,
                                         :ReactorBuildingContents ,
                                         [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                           owl:onProperty :RB4rNlczhggIx7lqdEK2d9c ;
                                           owl:someValuesFrom :ContainmentBuilding
                                         ] ,
                                         [ rdf:type owl:Restriction ;
                                           owl:onProperty :RB4rNlczhggIx7lqdEK2d9c ;
                                           owl:someValuesFrom :DryWell
                                         ] ;
                         <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "MSIV"@en ,
                                                                        "ReactorMainSteamIsolationValve"@en ;
                         <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> """The MSIVs are engineered safety features designed to limit the release of radioactive materials to the environment and to minimize reactor vessel inventory loss during a Design Basis Accident Redundant valves are located on each main steam line: one inside the drywell and one in the steam tunnel in the auxiliary building. The MSIVs are air- and spring-operated valves — air to open, air and spring to close .... (BWR)

The MSIVs are engineered safety features designed to limit the consequences of a steam line break. In the event of a steam line break inside containment, the MSIVs must close within 5 seconds to isolate the reverse steam flow from the intact SG to limit containment pressure below its design limits. The reverse steam flow is the result of the intact SG's feeding the steam line break through the pressure-equalizing header located in the turbine building. The containment is designed to accommodate only one SG blowdown... (PWR)"""@en ;
                         <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#prefLabel> "MainSteamIsolationValve"@en .

[ rdf:type owl:Axiom ;
   owl:annotatedSource :MainSteamIsolationValve ;
   owl:annotatedProperty <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> ;
   owl:annotatedTarget """The MSIVs are engineered safety features designed to limit the release of radioactive materials to the environment and to minimize reactor vessel inventory loss during a Design Basis Accident Redundant valves are located on each main steam line: one inside the drywell and one in the steam tunnel in the auxiliary building. The MSIVs are air- and spring-operated valves — air to open, air and spring to close .... (BWR)

The MSIVs are engineered safety features designed to limit the consequences of a steam line break. In the event of a steam line break inside containment, the MSIVs must close within 5 seconds to isolate the reverse steam flow from the intact SG to limit containment pressure below its design limits. The reverse steam flow is the result of the intact SG's feeding the steam line break through the pressure-equalizing header located in the turbine building. The containment is designed to accommodate only one SG blowdown... (PWR)"""@en ;
   :referenceINIS "https://inis.iaea.org/collection/NCLCollectionStore/_Public/27/051/27051618.pdf"
 ] .


